WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.896
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:08.766
[CLICKING]

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.100
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.125
So in this lecture,
we open a new chapter

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:20.850
of weak interaction.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.100
So we are one by
one adding together

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.290
the components we need
in order to describe

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.645
all elementary particles
and their interactions.

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:32.220
And I'll be adding the
third form of interaction.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.990
After the QED and QCD, we
enter into the discussion

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.850
of the weak interaction.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.010
So let's have a look
at the standard model.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.370
So we discussed gluons and QCD.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.170
And we saw that gluons
couple to themselves and also

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.130
to all quarks.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.677
Because they carry
a charge under the--

00:00:53.677 --> 00:00:57.260
on the QCD, a color charge.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.780
We have also
discussed the photon,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.960
and seen that the photon, they
do not couple to themselves,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.150
but they couple to all
charged elementary particles.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.840
Those are the meta
particles, the fermions.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.090
The photon also
couples to the W boson.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.720
We call it the
electrical charge.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.030
So now what we want to
do in this next chapter.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.690
We want to fully understand
the W and the Z boson.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.580
And we will see that they
couple to all meta particles.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.360
And we'll also discuss how they
might couple to themselves,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.840
or the Z boson couples
to the W boson.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.240
Well, that's the story
of this entire chapter,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.920
and we'll take it one by one.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.700
As an introduction, we start
with the Feynman Rules.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.940
So having the Feynman Rules
in place, and the cookbooks,

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.060
the recipe, in order to
calculate decays and scattering

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.260
processes.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.370
That is all we need in
order to get moving.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.620
You can, for example,
look at this vertex

00:01:54.620 --> 00:02:01.530
here, of this component
of the Feynman diagram.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.480
And what we need
to analyze this is

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.250
the propagator for the W and Z
boson, and the vertex factor.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.200
This vertex factor
now looks a little bit

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.560
more complicated
than for QED and QCD,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.840
because the W boson and the
Z boson, they carry mass.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.660
So we have some additional
factors. q squared minus M

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.530
square.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.720
And this q squared over
M squared term as well.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.010
One interesting fact
about this vertex factor

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.720
is what happens now, is q
squared is much, much smaller

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.180
than M squared.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.280
We have to get rid of
those components here,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.560
and we find a
vertex factor which

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.860
looks similar to the
one we have in QED.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.370
However, that's not one over
q squared term, but 1 over M

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.480
squared term, which is constant.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.030
So we would see
that we can describe

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.960
this in the context
of the Fermi theory,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.840
which is a lower
energy approximation

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.050
of the full theory
of heat conduction.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.360
It's kind of an
interesting concept,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.000
and it extends to the
entire understanding

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.350
of the standard model.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.610
It might be that
our standard model,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.940
you know, that we have
all the packages together,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.610
describes the lower
energy approximation

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:30.890
of a more complicated-- more
holistic theory, which we then

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:37.610
can discuss under the concept
of a grand unified theory.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.110
Maybe there's a
symmetry group which

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.340
is embedding the symmetry
groups we need for QED, QCD,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.750
and the weak interaction.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.850
But that's a side remark.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:54.510
So we will look at the Fermi
Theory a little bit more later.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.305
The vertex factor itself,
describing the vertex here.

00:03:57.305 --> 00:03:59.060
It's given here.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.083
For the W boson, and
also for the Z boson.

00:04:03.083 --> 00:04:05.500
And it looks a little bit more
complicated than the vertex

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.400
factors we have seen so far.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.810
What you notice that
there is the parameter,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.990
which is associated to the
strength of the interaction.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.200
And the gamma matrix.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.090
But there's also this term
here, which has two components.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.190
There's the one, and
the gamma 5 matrix.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.350
We have talked about
gamma 5 matrix already.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:36.130
And we can later identify
those as individual currents

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:42.040
are coupling to vector current
and an [INAUDIBLE] current.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.080
So this looks even
more complicated

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.600
now for the Z boson,
because here we

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:53.390
have not just numbers of one,
but an additional factor.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.920
This factor cV is
a vector coupling,

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.120
and it's specific
for each fermion.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.420
So each fermion has
one of those constants.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.920
And the second part of the
package or set of constants,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.500
for the axial current.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.490
You have a second
parameter here,

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.720
which is the strength of
the coupling of the Z boson.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.697
So at this point, you
just take this axial,

00:05:16.697 --> 00:05:18.155
and you can do all
our calculation.

00:05:18.155 --> 00:05:20.440
On our next slide,
I'm going to explain

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.640
to you what the corresponding
numbers and what d values are

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.280
for those parameters cV and cA.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.640
Later, we will see how it
comes to this more complicated

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.220
structure, and why
there is a vector,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.790
and why there is an
actual axial current

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:38.998
in the weak interaction.

00:05:38.998 --> 00:05:40.790
But for now, we just
take this for granted,

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:44.700
and we just take
this as a recipe.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:45.900
So now for the neutral.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.290
So we've just have seen that
this is the vertex factor.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.600
And here, for all
fermions, we list what

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.480
these values are for cV and cA.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.760
What you can see is
for the neutrinos,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.630
the factor is one half,
both for cV and for cA.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:08.650
And for the charge
leptons and quarks,

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.140
there is an even more
complicated term here,

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.120
which includes a new parameter.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.030
Sine squared theta w.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.490
The value of this is 28 degrees.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:25.220
Sine squared theta w is 0.231.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.770
As a little bit of a
preview here already,

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:32.300
the fact that there is this new
parameter and an angle involved

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.060
leads to, or can
be explained later,

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.900
by the fact that the weak
interaction is actually

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:45.490
a result of a mixing between
an original weak interaction,

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:46.680
and QED.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.180
So there's a mixed
thing going on.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.380
In other words,
the Z boson itself

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.520
is a mixture between
the thing which

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.950
couples to the weak
part of the particle,

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.980
and the part, which
couples to the electrically

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.390
charged part of the particle.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.720
When you see that,
that's why there

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:09.570
is a simple factor for the
neutrinos who are electrically

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:14.310
neutral, and a more complicated
term here for the electrically

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.270
charged particle.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.480
And you see that this is
the electric charge here.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.420
Or two times the electric
charge of the particles.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:27.970
But for now, those are all
just constants and recipes

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:29.290
to be used.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.020
One additional
word on the history

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.440
of the neutral charge of
the neutral weak current

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.540
is given here.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:41.290
So in the '60s and
'70s, the standard model

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:42.890
was slowly developed.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:46.150
A little bit more slowly than
we do in this class here.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.340
And there was--
the hypothesis is

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.740
that there have to be something
like a neutral current

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:51.430
in there.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.740
But it has never been
observed in nature.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:58.240
And so this bubble chamber,
specifically the one

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.330
Gargamelle at CERN, one was
able to actually see those, see

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:06.460
virtual and really
see, those interactions

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:07.390
for the first time.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:12.520
And the first pictures that have
been taken in the 1970s, 1973.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.410
And this picture
here illustrates--

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.660
I will expand it in a second--

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:19.120
illustrates the
interaction of a neutrino

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.320
coming into the bubble
chamber, making an interaction

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.470
with an electron, and then
scattering off, kicking off

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.240
the electron.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.160
So what you see here
is this incoming--

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.900
this is an anti-neutrino
kicking off an electron.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.740
See the electron here.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:38.875
The neutrino goes
off undetected.

00:08:38.875 --> 00:08:41.520
It just disappears.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.690
You see here, the electron.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.550
And then there's
also two protons.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:47.610
One proton here.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.030
Let's use a different color.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.680
And one photon here.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:56.270
Causing electron positron
pair, and the second photon

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.470
here doing the very same thing.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:02.040
You can see those
particles here.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.950
See here and then
going on here as well.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:08.630
So this is a bubble
chamber picture.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.620
We'll talk about bubble
chambers very briefly

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.890
later in the lecture as well.

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:16.160
But they're very extremely
important and useful tools

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.230
in order to illustrate--

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:23.670
to visualize and measure
particle interaction.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:24.170
All right.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.450
So much to the
introduction, and we'll

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.590
continue now with the
next lecture on talking

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:34.810
about this mixture, this
electroweak mixture [INAUDIBLE]

